// =============================================================================
// Connected Knowledge (epic #344)
// =============================================================================
//
// The graph foundation: entities, relations, items (commitments, decisions,
// claims, person facts), the evidence that anchors every claim back to a
// transcript segment or note span, and the review-and-commit proposal model
// extraction runs through. Full design in `docs/specs/ontology.md`; §10 is
// this section's own summary, kept in sync with the column list below.
// SCHEMA ONLY in this migration — no service, no endpoint, no data. The
// evidence-invariant trigger is #355's own migration, landing with the code
// that satisfies it; `kg_graph_layouts` and `ask_*` are #371's and #376's
// own migrations.
//
// Five conventions every model below shares, stated once here rather than
// repeated per model:
//
// 1. `owner_id → users.id`, `onDelete: Cascade`, on every table but one. An
//    entity/relation/item/evidence row/mention/proposal/merge/distinct-pair/
//    attribute-def has no meaning and no permission path to read it once its
//    owner is gone — the identical reasoning `notes.owner_id`/
//    `transcripts.owner_id` already establish, and there is no
//    `graph:read_any` for the identical reason there is no `notes:read_any`.
//    `kg_entity_views` is the one exception: it carries `user_id`, because
//    "which entities has THIS user looked at" is a per-viewer fact, not an
//    ownership fact.
// 2. `type`/`kind` columns that name an ONTOLOGY key (`kg_entities.type`,
//    `kg_relations.type`) are plain TEXT, never a Prisma enum — the same
//    "small, code-owned, growing set" reasoning `jobs.type` and
//    `storage_objects.managed_by` already establish elsewhere in this
//    schema. Every enum below instead closes a set this SCHEMA itself
//    enforces (a review lifecycle, a proposal's own status machine) — see
//    §17.4 for the full type-vs-enum argument.
// 3. `props`/`stats`/`user_guidance` JSONB columns default `'{}'` where
//    listed, exactly like `jobs.payload`/`kg_entities.props` elsewhere in
//    this file — an empty object, never NULL, so a reader never has to
//    special-case "no extra data yet" against "extra data exists but is
//    empty."
// 4. `ontology_version` (text) on every row a §17.4 definition-file version
//    could apply to (`kg_entities`, `kg_relations`, `kg_items`) — which
//    version of the ontology's own type/attribute definitions this row was
//    written against, so a later definition-file migration can tell an
//    old row from a new one without re-deriving it.
// 5. `valid tstzrange` (on `kg_relations` and `kg_items`) is written and
//    read entirely OUTSIDE Prisma's query API. Prisma cannot construct,
//    bind, or select a `tstzrange` value — the column is declared
//    `Unsupported("tstzrange")` below specifically so the generated client
//    refuses to touch it at all, the same "declare it unsupported so a
//    mistaken ORM write is impossible" shape `SearchEmbedding.embedding`
//    already establishes for `vector(1536)`. Writers set `valid` with
//    `$executeRaw` inside the SAME transaction as the `create`; readers
//    select `lower(valid)`, `upper(valid)` and `lower_inc(valid)`/
//    `upper_inc(valid)` via `$queryRaw`. #353 provides the `toPgRange`/
//    `fromPgRange` helpers every future reader/writer uses instead of
//    reinventing this.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THERE IS NO ANCHOR CHECK ON `kg_evidence`.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every FK `kg_evidence` holds into another module's table
// (`transcript_id`/`segment_id`/`note_id`/`import_object_id`) is `SetNull`,
// by design (§10) — an evidence row is a CITATION, and a citation survives
// losing the thing it pointed to; `quote` (NOT NULL) is stored precisely so
// the citation stays readable once every anchor has gone to NULL. A CHECK
// requiring at least one anchor to be non-NULL would make deleting a
// transcript FAIL the moment its own evidence rows lose their last pointer —
// exactly the failure `SetNull` exists to avoid. So there is deliberately no
// such CHECK: a fully-anchorless evidence row (every FK NULL, `quote` intact)
// is a valid, expected end state, not a bug to prevent.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// INTENTIONAL SCHEMA DRIFT — HAND-WRITTEN, PRISMA-INEXPRESSIBLE SQL LIVES
// ONLY IN `migration.sql`, THE SAME PATTERN `jobs_active_dedup_uniq_idx` AND
// `search_embeddings_embedding_hnsw_idx` ALREADY ESTABLISH.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `migration.sql` for this section hand-writes:
//   - `CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_trgm` — a new capability for this
//     codebase (pgvector already is not new; `SearchEmbedding` already
//     depends on it). Trusted on PG13+, so the migration role needs no
//     superuser.
//   - Two `gin (… gin_trgm_ops)` trigram indexes (fuzzy candidate generation,
//     §7): `kg_entities_label_trgm_idx`, `kg_entity_aliases_normalized_trgm_idx`.
//   - Two `gist (valid)` range indexes (§5.4's containment/overlap queries):
//     `kg_relations_valid_gist_idx`, `kg_items_valid_gist_idx`.
//   - Two `hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops)` indexes, matching
//     `SearchEmbedding`'s own hand-written index discipline exactly:
//     `kg_entities_embedding_hnsw_idx`, `kg_items_embedding_hnsw_idx`.
//   - Five partial unique indexes and one CHECK-shaped guard, all below.
//   - Eight CHECK constraints, all below.
// None of the above has a Prisma DSL equivalent; `prisma migrate dev`/`diff`
// will propose to drop every one of them and must never be asked to
// reconcile that "drift" away.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FIVE PARTIAL UNIQUE INDEXES, EACH ENFORCED IN THE DATABASE, NEVER BY A
// `findFirst` BEFORE THE INSERT — THE `database_backup_runs_active_uniq_idx`
// REASONING, FIVE TIMES OVER.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   - `kg_items_live_statement_uniq_idx` — the "known, skipped" dedup guard
//     (§8): a verbatim restatement of an already-`accepted`/`edited` item
//     attaches new evidence instead of inserting a duplicate row. Scoped to
//     `review_status IN ('accepted','edited')` and `subject_id IS NOT NULL`
//     so a `rejected` row's hash never blocks a legitimate later insert.
//   - `kg_proposals_note_draft_uniq_idx` — at most one OPEN draft per note.
//     Whoever creates a new draft discards the previous one in the same
//     transaction (#363); this index is what makes "at most one" true even
//     under a race, not the service-layer discard alone.
//   - `kg_proposals_note_extracting_uniq_idx` — at most one RUNNING
//     extraction per note (#363's 409 `extraction_running`).
//   - `kg_proposals_owner_import_extracting_uniq_idx` — at most one RUNNING
//     import per OWNER (#387's), scoped by `owner_id` rather than `note_id`
//     because an import proposal has no note at all.
//   - `kg_relations_speaker_link_uniq_idx` — at most one `IDENTIFIED_AS`
//     edge per diarized speaker (a speaker names at most one entity).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// EIGHT CHECK CONSTRAINTS.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   - `kg_distinct_pairs_order_chk` (`a_id < b_id`) — canonicalizes an
//     unordered pair into exactly one storable row, so `(a, b)` and `(b, a)`
//     can never both be inserted as two different "confirmed distinct" facts.
//   - `kg_mentions_one_source_chk` (`num_nonnulls(note_id, transcript_id) =
//     1`) — a mention always comes from exactly one source document.
//   - `kg_relations_one_source_chk` (`num_nonnulls(from_id, from_speaker_id)
//     = 1`) — a relation's `from` side is EITHER a resolved entity OR a
//     diarized speaker awaiting `IDENTIFIED_AS`, never both, never neither.
//   - `kg_relations_speaker_type_chk` (`from_speaker_id IS NULL OR type =
//     'IDENTIFIED_AS'`) — `from_speaker_id` exists for exactly one
//     relation type, and no other.
//   - `kg_relations_valid_precision_chk` (`(valid IS NULL) =
//     (valid_precision IS NULL) OR valid_precision IS NOT DISTINCT FROM
//     'unknown'` — `IS NOT DISTINCT FROM`, not `=`, since #398: with `=` a
//     NULL precision made the CHECK evaluate NULL, which passes) — a
//     temporal relation always states a precision, but `unknown` is
//     representable even should `valid` itself later become NULL (e.g. a
//     future correction that clears a range while a precision is still on
//     record as having once been claimed).
//   - `kg_items_sensitivity_chk` (`(kind = 'person_fact') = (sensitivity IS
//     NOT NULL)`) — sensitivity is meaningful for exactly one item kind
//     (§5.6), and this makes "a claim with a sensitivity" and "a person
//     fact with none" both unrepresentable rather than merely discouraged.
//   - `kg_items_subject_required_chk` (`kind NOT IN ('claim','person_fact')
//     OR subject_id IS NOT NULL`) — a claim or a person fact is always
//     ABOUT something; a commitment/decision may stand on its own.
//   - `kg_evidence_char_range_chk` / `kg_evidence_ms_range_chk` — an end
//     offset, when both ends are given, is never before its start.
//   - `kg_proposal_items_kind_payload_chk` (`jsonb_typeof(payload) =
//     'object'`) — `payload` is always a JSON object, never a bare array or
//     scalar, so every reader can assume key access without a type guard.
//   - `kg_proposal_items_merge_into_chk` (`merge_into_id IS NULL OR decision
//     = 'merge_into'`) — the override column and the decision it overrides
//     can never disagree about which decision this row represents.
//     `merge_into_id` is `SetNull`: forgetting or purging the target entity
//     clears the override, and the row falls back to `pending` semantics at
//     commit (#366 refuses a `merge_into` row without a target).
//   - `kg_proposals_note_source_chk` (`CASE WHEN kind = 'extraction' THEN
//     note_version IS NOT NULL ELSE note_id IS NULL AND note_version IS
//     NULL END`) — keys on `note_version`, NEVER on `note_id`, and this is
//     deliberate: `note_id` is inserted non-null for every extraction
//     proposal and can only become NULL afterwards through the note's own
//     `SetNull` FK once the note is hard-deleted. A CHECK keyed on `note_id`
//     would then start failing for rows that were valid when written; a
//     CHECK keyed on `note_version` (never nulled by any FK action) stays
//     satisfied for the life of the row.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FUTURE MIGRATIONS: when a later `prisma migrate dev` proposes `DROP INDEX`
// for any hand-written name above, DELETE those proposed lines. This is
// intentional drift, not decay to fix.
// =============================================================================

enum KgReviewStatus {
  unreviewed
  accepted
  edited
  rejected
  merged
  superseded

  @@map("kg_review_status")
}

enum KgValidPrecision {
  day
  month
  year
  unknown

  @@map("kg_valid_precision")
}

enum KgItemKind {
  commitment
  decision
  claim
  person_fact
  risk

  @@map("kg_item_kind")
}

enum KgSensitivity {
  business
  personal
  sensitive

  @@map("kg_sensitivity")
}

enum KgAliasSource {
  user
  extraction
  speaker_naming
  import

  @@map("kg_alias_source")
}

enum KgEvidenceSubjectKind {
  entity
  relation
  item
  proposal_item
  import

  @@map("kg_evidence_subject_kind")
}

enum KgMentionStatus {
  suggested
  linked
  new
  ignored

  @@map("kg_mention_status")
}
// The COMPLETE proposal model, up front — every status and kind #363, #364,
// #366 and #387 need, so none of them ever needs `ALTER TYPE … ADD VALUE` or
// a proposal-table migration of its own. `extracting`/`resolution` are used
// by no code yet; #363 owns the prose for the `extracting` lifecycle and the
// `resolution` kind, this migration owns only the column list (issue #351).
enum KgProposalKind {
  extraction
  import
  resolution

  @@map("kg_proposal_kind")
}
// Lifecycle: `extracting → draft | failed`, `draft → committed | discarded`,
// `failed → discarded`, `committed → reverted`. Transitions are enforced by
// the SERVICES (#363/#366), not by a database trigger — this enum only
// closes the set of states a row may ever be in.
enum KgProposalStatus {
  draft
  extracting
  committed
  discarded
  failed
  reverted

  @@map("kg_proposal_status")
}

enum KgProposalItemKind {
  entity
  relation
  item
  closing

  @@map("kg_proposal_item_kind")
}

enum KgProposalItemDecision {
  pending
  accept
  edit
  reject
  merge_into

  @@map("kg_proposal_item_decision")
}

enum KgProposalItemOrigin {
  ai
  user

  @@map("kg_proposal_item_origin")
}

enum KgAttributeKind {
  text
  number
  date
  boolean
  select
  multi_select
  url
  entity_ref

  @@map("kg_attribute_kind")
}
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `kg_entities` — Person, Organization, Project, Meeting (`type` is a plain
// ontology key, §17.4 — never a Prisma enum; see the section header above).
// `owner_id` Cascade — see convention 1 above. `embedding vector(1536)`
// reuses the EXACT model/dimension contract `SearchEmbedding` already
// carries (see that model's own block comment for the full "why
// `Unsupported`, why raw SQL" argument) rather than inventing a second
// embedding convention; `embedding_hash` is the content hash #364's
// `kg.embed` keys re-embedding on, mirroring `SearchChunk.contentHash`.
// `merged_into_id` is a self-relation: once an entity is merged into
// another (§7), this points at the survivor and `review_status` moves to
// `merged` — SetNull so deleting the survivor un-merges rather than
// cascading a delete backward onto every entity it absorbed.
// `occurred_at` is set on `Meeting` rows only (the ontology's own type
// contract, not a database constraint — a fork's own type could reuse it
// differently).
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
model KgEntity {
  id String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid

  ownerId String @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid
  type    String
  label   String
  props   Json   @default("{}") @db.JsonB

  // See the section header's convention 5 — `Unsupported`, written/read only
  // through `$executeRaw`/`$queryRaw`, matching `SearchEmbedding.embedding`.
  // Written as `Unsupported("vector(1536)")` with the parentheses as \u0028/\u0029: the
  // composer's field reader (platform-db 0.1.0-next.5) stops a type at the first `)`.
  // Prisma decodes the escapes, so the column type is still vector(1536).
  embedding      Unsupported("vector\u00281536\u0029")?
  embeddingModel String?                      @map("embedding_model")
  embeddingHash  String?                      @map("embedding_hash")

  reviewStatus KgReviewStatus @default(accepted) @map("review_status")
  mergedIntoId String?        @map("merged_into_id") @db.Uuid
  occurredAt   DateTime?      @map("occurred_at") @db.Timestamptz

  ontologyVersion String   @map("ontology_version")
  createdAt       DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt       DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  owner      User      @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  mergedInto KgEntity? @relation("KgEntityMergedInto", fields: [mergedIntoId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  mergedFrom              KgEntity[]        @relation("KgEntityMergedInto")
  aliases                 KgEntityAlias[]
  relationsFrom           KgRelation[]      @relation("KgRelationFrom")
  relationsTo             KgRelation[]      @relation("KgRelationTo")
  itemsAsSubject          KgItem[]          @relation("KgItemSubject")
  itemsAsMeeting          KgItem[]          @relation("KgItemMeeting")
  itemsAsOwnerPerson      KgItem[]          @relation("KgItemOwnerPerson")
  itemsAsCounterparty     KgItem[]          @relation("KgItemCounterparty")
  mentions                KgMention[]
  proposalItemsMergedInto KgProposalItem[]  @relation("KgProposalItemMergeInto")
  mergesAsSurvivor        KgMerge[]         @relation("KgMergeSurvivor")
  mergesAsMerged          KgMerge[]         @relation("KgMergeMerged")
  distinctPairsAsA        KgDistinctPair[]  @relation("KgDistinctPairA")
  distinctPairsAsB        KgDistinctPair[]  @relation("KgDistinctPairB")
  digest                  KgEntityDigest?
  views                   KgEntityView[]
  // Ask conversations scoped to this entity (issue #376). SetNull on the
  // conversation's side — forgetting or merging a person drops the scope
  // link, never the user's own conversation.
  askConversationsScoped  AskConversation[]

  // "This owner's entities of type X, by review state" and "this owner's
  // entities, recently updated" — the two shapes an entity list/filter reads.
  @@index([ownerId, type, reviewStatus])
  @@index([ownerId, updatedAt(sort: Desc)])
  // "Every entity that merged into this one" — the merge-reversal read path.
  @@index([mergedIntoId])
  // HAND-WRITTEN in migration.sql only: `kg_entities_label_trgm_idx` (gin,
  // gin_trgm_ops) and `kg_entities_embedding_hnsw_idx` (hnsw,
  // vector_cosine_ops). See the section header's "INTENTIONAL SCHEMA DRIFT"
  // note — Prisma has no DSL for either.
  @@map("kg_entities")
}
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `kg_entity_aliases` — a SEPARATE table, not an array column on
// `kg_entities`, specifically because an alias needs its own indexed
// exact/fuzzy lookup AND its own provenance (`source`) — an array column
// could hold the strings but not which of four very different origins each
// one came from (§10). `normalized` is produced by #355's `normalizeAlias()`
// (NFKC, lowercase, trimmed, whitespace collapsed) — this REPLACES `citext`,
// which is not installed and is not needed: a normalized `text` column
// produced by one pure function gives exact matching AND feeds `pg_trgm`
// directly, at the cost of nothing `citext` would have bought here.
// `entity_id`/`owner_id` both Cascade — an alias has no meaning once either
// its entity or its owner is gone.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
model KgEntityAlias {
  id       String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  entityId String @map("entity_id") @db.Uuid
  ownerId  String @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid

  alias      String
  // See the block comment above the model for the full `normalizeAlias()`
  // argument — this is why there is no `citext` column here.
  normalized String

  source KgAliasSource

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz

  entity KgEntity @relation(fields: [entityId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  owner  User     @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  // One alias per (entity, normalized form) — a repeat submission of the
  // identically-normalized alias is a no-op, not a duplicate row.
  @@unique([entityId, normalized])
  @@index([ownerId, normalized])
  @@index([entityId])
  // HAND-WRITTEN in migration.sql only: `kg_entity_aliases_normalized_trgm_idx`
  // (gin, gin_trgm_ops) — fuzzy candidate generation (§7). See the section
  // header's "INTENTIONAL SCHEMA DRIFT" note.
  @@map("kg_entity_aliases")
}
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `kg_relations` — `type` is one of §5.2's sixteen ontology edge types
// (plain text, never a Prisma enum — convention 2 above). No inverse row is
// EVER stored (§5.2): a "from" side is either a resolved `KgEntity`
// (`from_id`) or a diarized speaker awaiting identification
// (`from_speaker_id`) — `kg_relations_one_source_chk` makes exactly one of
// the two representable, and `kg_relations_speaker_type_chk` restricts
// `from_speaker_id` to the one relation type (`IDENTIFIED_AS`) whose source
// is a speaker rather than an entity. This is a contract addition beyond
// spec §10's original text (§5.1/§5.2): a polymorphic `from_kind`/`from_id`
// pair without FKs was rejected in favour of a REAL FK to
// `transcript_speakers`, so deleting a merged speaker's row cleans up the
// edge for free. `valid`/`valid_precision` are the §5.4 bitemporal pair —
// see the section header's convention 5 for how `valid` is actually
// written/read. `superseded_by_id` is a self-relation (§5.5's "closing a
// temporal edge" case), SetNull so deleting the successor un-supersedes
// rather than cascading backward.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
model KgRelation {
  id      String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  ownerId String @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid
  type    String

  // Endpoints — see the block comment above the model for the full
  // exactly-one-of argument enforced by `kg_relations_one_source_chk`.
  fromId        String? @map("from_id") @db.Uuid
  fromSpeakerId String? @map("from_speaker_id") @db.Uuid
  toId          String  @map("to_id") @db.Uuid

  props Json @default("{}") @db.JsonB

  // §5.4's bitemporal pair. See the section header's convention 5 for why
  // `valid` is `Unsupported` and written/read only via raw SQL.
  valid          Unsupported("tstzrange")?
  validPrecision KgValidPrecision?         @map("valid_precision")

  reviewStatus   KgReviewStatus @default(accepted) @map("review_status")
  confidence     Float?
  supersededById String?        @map("superseded_by_id") @db.Uuid

  ontologyVersion String   @map("ontology_version")
  createdAt       DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt       DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  owner        User               @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  fromEntity   KgEntity?          @relation("KgRelationFrom", fields: [fromId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  fromSpeaker  TranscriptSpeaker? @relation(fields: [fromSpeakerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  toEntity     KgEntity           @relation("KgRelationTo", fields: [toId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  supersededBy KgRelation?        @relation("KgRelationSupersededBy", fields: [supersededById], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  supersedes KgRelation[] @relation("KgRelationSupersededBy")

  // §5.2's "joins are symmetric" argument, made concrete: a relation is
  // looked up from either endpoint equally often.
  @@index([ownerId, fromId, type])
  @@index([ownerId, toId, type])
  @@index([fromSpeakerId])
  @@index([supersededById])
  @@index([ownerId, type, reviewStatus])
  // HAND-WRITTEN in migration.sql only: `kg_relations_valid_gist_idx` (gist,
  // §5.4's containment/overlap queries) and `kg_relations_speaker_link_uniq_idx`
  // (a partial unique index — see the section header). See the section
  // header's "INTENTIONAL SCHEMA DRIFT" note.
  @@map("kg_relations")
}
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `kg_items` — Commitment, Decision, Claim, and PersonFact in ONE table,
// distinguished by `kind` (a real Prisma enum here, unlike `kg_entities
// .type`/`kg_relations.type` — see §10, this is the schema's own closed
// four-way split, not an open ontology key). `meeting_id` backs
// `CREATED_IN`/`DECIDED_IN` (a contract addition beyond the original §10
// text) — SetNull, so deleting the meeting entity leaves the item itself
// intact with a now-unknown venue rather than deleting the item.
// `owner_person_id`/`counterparty_id` Cascade (deleting the person this
// commitment is FOR/BETWEEN removes the commitment's meaning entirely, the
// same argument as `subject_id`); `subject_id` also Cascade. `sensitivity`
// is meaningful for exactly one `kind` (§5.6), enforced by
// `kg_items_sensitivity_chk`. `statement_hash` is the §7 dedup/suppression
// key `kg_items_live_statement_uniq_idx` enforces. `valid`/`valid_precision`
// mirror `kg_relations`'s own bitemporal pair (§5.4) so an item's own valid
// period is never a second stored shape from its edges' shape.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
model KgItem {
  id      String     @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  ownerId String     @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid
  kind    KgItemKind

  subjectId      String? @map("subject_id") @db.Uuid
  meetingId      String? @map("meeting_id") @db.Uuid
  ownerPersonId  String? @map("owner_person_id") @db.Uuid
  counterpartyId String? @map("counterparty_id") @db.Uuid

  title     String?
  // Commitment: `open | done | dropped | superseded`. Others: `active |
  // superseded`. Plain text, not an enum — the same "small, code-owned set
  // that varies per `kind`" reasoning `props`/§10 already give this table
  // its single-table shape for.
  statement String
  status    String
  props     Json    @default("{}") @db.JsonB

  occurredAt DateTime? @map("occurred_at") @db.Timestamptz
  dueAt      DateTime? @map("due_at") @db.Timestamptz

  supersededById String?        @map("superseded_by_id") @db.Uuid
  sensitivity    KgSensitivity?
  // The §7 dedup/suppression key — see `kg_items_live_statement_uniq_idx`
  // below (hand-written, since Prisma cannot express its `WHERE` clause).
  statementHash  String         @map("statement_hash")

  // §5.4's bitemporal pair, mirroring `kg_relations` exactly. See the
  // section header's convention 5 for why `valid` is `Unsupported`.
  valid          Unsupported("tstzrange")?
  validPrecision KgValidPrecision?         @map("valid_precision")

  // See the section header's convention 5 — same `Unsupported`/raw-SQL
  // contract as `kg_entities.embedding` and `SearchEmbedding.embedding`.
  // Written as `Unsupported("vector(1536)")` with the parentheses as \u0028/\u0029: the
  // composer's field reader (platform-db 0.1.0-next.5) stops a type at the first `)`.
  // Prisma decodes the escapes, so the column type is still vector(1536).
  embedding      Unsupported("vector\u00281536\u0029")?
  embeddingModel String?                      @map("embedding_model")
  embeddingHash  String?                      @map("embedding_hash")

  reviewStatus KgReviewStatus @default(accepted) @map("review_status")
  confidence   Float?

  ontologyVersion String   @map("ontology_version")
  createdAt       DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt       DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  owner        User      @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  subject      KgEntity? @relation("KgItemSubject", fields: [subjectId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  meeting      KgEntity? @relation("KgItemMeeting", fields: [meetingId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  ownerPerson  KgEntity? @relation("KgItemOwnerPerson", fields: [ownerPersonId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  counterparty KgEntity? @relation("KgItemCounterparty", fields: [counterpartyId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  supersededBy KgItem?   @relation("KgItemSupersededBy", fields: [supersededById], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  supersedes KgItem[] @relation("KgItemSupersededBy")

  // §9.1's "what's changed for this entity" brief query.
  @@index([ownerId, subjectId, occurredAt(sort: Desc)])
  @@index([ownerId, kind, status])
  @@index([ownerId, statementHash])
  @@index([ownerPersonId])
  @@index([counterpartyId])
  @@index([meetingId])
  @@index([supersededById])
  // HAND-WRITTEN in migration.sql only: `kg_items_valid_gist_idx` (gist),
  // `kg_items_embedding_hnsw_idx` (hnsw, vector_cosine_ops), and
  // `kg_items_live_statement_uniq_idx` (a partial unique index — the "known,
  // skipped" §8 dedup guard). See the section header's "INTENTIONAL SCHEMA
  // DRIFT" note.
  @@map("kg_items")
}
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `kg_evidence` — the §5.3 evidence contract. `subject_kind`/`subject_id`
// are POLYMORPHIC, carrying NO foreign key — the identical `jobs.subject_
// type`/`subject_id` pattern this schema already uses, because the set of
// things evidence can anchor (an entity, a relation, an item, a proposal
// item, an import) is open-ended and code-owned. The transcript/note/import
// anchors ARE real foreign keys, and every one of them is `SetNull` — see
// the section header's "WHY THERE IS NO ANCHOR CHECK" note for the full
// argument; `quote` (NOT NULL) is what keeps a citation readable once every
// anchor has gone to NULL. `char_start`/`char_end` are offsets into the
// cited NOTE VERSION BODY when `note_id` is set, or into the cited
// SEGMENT'S TEXT when `segment_id` is set — NULL means the whole segment.
// On a NOTE citation, both NULL means "the note's Context field" (#440): the
// Context has no body offsets, so the quote alone carries the span.
// #363 relies on this exact convention; do not reinterpret it per-caller.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
model KgEvidence {
  id          String                @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  ownerId     String                @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid
  subjectKind KgEvidenceSubjectKind @map("subject_kind")
  // Polymorphic, no FK — see the block comment above the model.
  subjectId   String                @map("subject_id") @db.Uuid

  // Transcript anchor. SetNull — see the section header's "WHY THERE IS NO
  // ANCHOR CHECK" note.
  transcriptId String? @map("transcript_id") @db.Uuid
  segmentId    String? @map("segment_id") @db.Uuid
  segmentRev   Int?    @map("segment_rev")
  startMs      Int?    @map("start_ms")
  endMs        Int?    @map("end_ms")

  // Note anchor. SetNull, same reasoning.
  noteId      String? @map("note_id") @db.Uuid
  noteVersion Int?    @map("note_version")

  // Offsets into the cited NOTE VERSION BODY when `noteId` is set, or into
  // the cited SEGMENT'S TEXT when `segmentId` is set. NULL means the whole
  // segment; on a note citation, both NULL means the note's Context field
  // (#440). See the block comment above the model — #363 reads this
  // convention directly.
  charStart Int? @map("char_start")
  charEnd   Int? @map("char_end")

  // The text AT ANCHORING TIME (§5.3) — NOT NULL, deliberately: this is
  // what keeps the citation readable once every anchor above has gone to
  // NULL (see the section header's "WHY THERE IS NO ANCHOR CHECK" note).
  quote String

  // Import anchor. SetNull, same reasoning.
  importObjectId String? @map("import_object_id") @db.Uuid
  sourceIri      String? @map("source_iri")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz

  owner        User               @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  transcript   Transcript?        @relation(fields: [transcriptId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  segment      TranscriptSegment? @relation(fields: [segmentId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  note         Note?              @relation(fields: [noteId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  importObject StorageObject?     @relation(fields: [importObjectId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@index([subjectKind, subjectId])
  @@index([ownerId, transcriptId])
  @@index([segmentId])
  @@index([ownerId, noteId])
  @@map("kg_evidence")
}
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `kg_mentions` — the coarse `MENTIONS` shortcut (§5.2): what an entity
// page's "notes about Joe" section reads, distinct from `kg_evidence`'s
// precise per-claim citation. Exactly one source document per row
// (`kg_mentions_one_source_chk`). `entity_id`/`note_id`/`transcript_id` ALL
// Cascade — a mention has no meaning once either side is gone, the opposite
// of `kg_evidence`'s pointer relationship above.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
model KgMention {
  id      String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  ownerId String @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid

  entityId String @map("entity_id") @db.Uuid

  noteId       String? @map("note_id") @db.Uuid
  transcriptId String? @map("transcript_id") @db.Uuid

  // `{charStart,charEnd}` or `{segmentId}` — which shape depends on which of
  // `noteId`/`transcriptId` is set.
  span   Json?           @db.JsonB
  status KgMentionStatus @default(linked)
  score  Float?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz

  owner      User        @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  entity     KgEntity    @relation(fields: [entityId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  note       Note?       @relation(fields: [noteId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  transcript Transcript? @relation(fields: [transcriptId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@index([ownerId, entityId])
  @@index([noteId])
  @@index([transcriptId])
  // HAND-WRITTEN in migration.sql only: `kg_mentions_one_source_chk`. See
  // the section header's "EIGHT CHECK CONSTRAINTS" note.
  @@map("kg_mentions")
}
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `kg_proposals` — one row per extraction, import, or resolution run
// (§8, §18.3; the complete kind/status enums are declared above THIS
// migration, issue #351, so #363/#364/#366/#387 never need `ALTER TYPE`).
// Only `kind = 'extraction'` has a note — `kg_proposals_note_source_chk`
// requires `note_version` for `extraction` and requires BOTH `note_id` AND
// `note_version` NULL for `import`/`resolution`. The CHECK keys on
// `note_version`, never `note_id`: `note_id` is inserted non-null for every
// extraction and can only become NULL afterwards via the note's own
// `SetNull` FK once the note is hard-deleted, so a CHECK on `note_id` would
// start failing for rows that were valid when written. `commit_log` is
// #366's internal undo record — the commit writes it, the revert reads it —
// and it is NEVER serialized to clients; `stats` is the display copy.
// `job_id` mirrors `TranscriptExport.jobId` exactly (`@unique`, nullable,
// SetNull): a proposal's own review lifetime is independent of
// `job.history.purge`'s schedule for the job row.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
model KgProposal {
  id      String           @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  ownerId String           @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid
  kind    KgProposalKind
  status  KgProposalStatus @default(draft)

  // Only `kind = 'extraction'` has a note. See the block comment above the
  // model for why `kg_proposals_note_source_chk` keys on `noteVersion`.
  noteId      String? @map("note_id") @db.Uuid
  noteVersion Int?    @map("note_version")

  // Generation context — which task-model resolution actually ran (§20),
  // recorded rather than re-derived, so an administrator changing the
  // default tomorrow never rewrites what an already-committed proposal used
  // yesterday.
  model        String?
  provider     String?
  systemPrompt String? @map("system_prompt")
  userContent  String? @map("user_content")
  userGuidance Json?   @map("user_guidance") @db.JsonB

  stats Json @default("{}") @db.JsonB

  committedAt DateTime? @map("committed_at") @db.Timestamptz
  revertedAt  DateTime? @map("reverted_at") @db.Timestamptz
  jobId       String?   @unique @map("job_id") @db.Uuid

  // #366's internal undo record — the commit writes it, the revert reads
  // it. NEVER serialized to clients; `stats` above is the display copy.
  commitLog Json? @map("commit_log") @db.JsonB

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  owner User  @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  note  Note? @relation(fields: [noteId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  job   Job?  @relation(fields: [jobId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  items KgProposalItem[]

  @@index([ownerId, status, createdAt(sort: Desc)])
  @@index([noteId, createdAt(sort: Desc)])
  // HAND-WRITTEN in migration.sql only: `kg_proposals_note_draft_uniq_idx`,
  // `kg_proposals_note_extracting_uniq_idx`,
  // `kg_proposals_owner_import_extracting_uniq_idx` (three partial unique
  // indexes) and `kg_proposals_note_source_chk`. See the section header's
  // "FIVE PARTIAL UNIQUE INDEXES" / "EIGHT CHECK CONSTRAINTS" notes.
  @@map("kg_proposals")
}
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `kg_proposal_items` — one row per proposed entity, relation, item, or
// closing edit (`closing` is §7's "closing a temporal edge is a proposal
// row" case). `merge_into_id`/`distinct_from` are #366's reviewer-override
// columns, declared up front by this migration (issue #351) so #366 needs
// no schema change of its own: `merge_into_id` is set IFF `decision =
// 'merge_into'` (`kg_proposal_items_merge_into_chk`), SetNull so losing the
// target entity clears the override rather than blocking the delete;
// `distinct_from` records the "not the same as" candidates a reviewer ruled
// out, as plain uuids (no FK — these are recorded facts about a review
// decision, not live references another table's delete must cascade
// through). `committed_ref_id` is polymorphic (no FK), the row this item
// became once committed — #366's revert reads this to know exactly what to
// undo.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
model KgProposalItem {
  id         String             @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  proposalId String             @map("proposal_id") @db.Uuid
  kind       KgProposalItemKind

  payload       Json  @db.JsonB
  resolution    Json? @db.JsonB
  editedPayload Json? @map("edited_payload") @db.JsonB

  decision KgProposalItemDecision @default(pending)
  // Values from #363's `PROPOSAL_ITEM_FLAGS` — plain text[], not an enum:
  // the review panel's pre-check flags are a growing, code-owned set, the
  // same reasoning `kg_entities.type` stays plain text.
  flags    String[]               @default([])
  origin   KgProposalItemOrigin   @default(ai)

  // #366's reviewer-override columns. See the block comment above the model
  // for the exactly-one-decision argument `kg_proposal_items_merge_into_chk`
  // enforces, and why `mergeIntoId` is SetNull.
  mergeIntoId  String?  @map("merge_into_id") @db.Uuid
  distinctFrom String[] @default([]) @map("distinct_from")

  // Polymorphic, no FK — the `kg_entities`/`kg_relations`/`kg_items` row
  // this item became once committed.
  committedRefId String? @map("committed_ref_id") @db.Uuid
  sortOrder      Int     @default(0) @map("sort_order")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  proposal  KgProposal @relation(fields: [proposalId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  mergeInto KgEntity?  @relation("KgProposalItemMergeInto", fields: [mergeIntoId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@index([proposalId, kind, sortOrder])
  // HAND-WRITTEN in migration.sql only: `kg_proposal_items_kind_payload_chk`
  // and `kg_proposal_items_merge_into_chk`. See the section header's "EIGHT
  // CHECK CONSTRAINTS" note.
  @@map("kg_proposal_items")
}
// One row per merge (§7), with the full reversal payload — what #366 (and
// an entity page's "undo merge" action) replays to restore the merged
// entity. `survivor_id`/`merged_id` both Cascade: a merge record has no
// meaning once either entity it names is gone.
model KgMerge {
  id         String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  ownerId    String @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid
  survivorId String @map("survivor_id") @db.Uuid
  mergedId   String @map("merged_id") @db.Uuid

  reversal   Json      @db.JsonB
  reversedAt DateTime? @map("reversed_at") @db.Timestamptz

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz

  owner    User     @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  survivor KgEntity @relation("KgMergeSurvivor", fields: [survivorId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  merged   KgEntity @relation("KgMergeMerged", fields: [mergedId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@index([ownerId, createdAt(sort: Desc)])
  @@index([survivorId])
  @@index([mergedId])
  @@map("kg_merges")
}
// Confirmed-NOT-the-same pairs (§7), skipped by every future
// candidate-generation pass. `a_id < b_id` is enforced by
// `kg_distinct_pairs_order_chk` (hand-written — Prisma cannot express a
// cross-column CHECK), so `(a, b)` and `(b, a)` can never both exist as two
// different rows for what is the same unordered fact. Both entity FKs
// Cascade.
model KgDistinctPair {
  ownerId String @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid
  aId     String @map("a_id") @db.Uuid
  bId     String @map("b_id") @db.Uuid

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz

  owner   User     @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  entityA KgEntity @relation("KgDistinctPairA", fields: [aId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  entityB KgEntity @relation("KgDistinctPairB", fields: [bId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@id([ownerId, aId, bId])
  // HAND-WRITTEN in migration.sql only: `kg_distinct_pairs_order_chk`. See
  // the section header's "EIGHT CHECK CONSTRAINTS" note.
  @@map("kg_distinct_pairs")
}
// One row per user-defined attribute (§17.3) — the schema half of the
// ontology's user-extensibility story. `owner_id` Cascade, the same
// reasoning every other `kg_*` table's `owner_id` follows.
model KgAttributeDef {
  id      String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  ownerId String @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid

  entityType String          @map("entity_type")
  key        String
  label      String
  kind       KgAttributeKind
  options    Json?           @db.JsonB

  extractable    Boolean @default(false)
  extractionHint String? @map("extraction_hint")

  sensitivity  KgSensitivity?
  sortOrder    Int            @default(0) @map("sort_order")
  deprecatedAt DateTime?      @map("deprecated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  owner User @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([ownerId, entityType, key])
  @@index([ownerId, entityType, sortOrder])
  @@map("kg_attribute_defs")
}
// §9.2's precomputed entity brief — "precompute once, read cheaply," the
// same economy `kg_graph_layouts` (its own, later migration) applies to a
// whole-graph layout. `entity_id` is the PRIMARY KEY (not a separate `id`):
// there is exactly one digest per entity, always replaced in place, never
// versioned.
model KgEntityDigest {
  entityId String @id @map("entity_id") @db.Uuid
  ownerId  String @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid

  summary     String
  citations   Json     @db.JsonB
  coversUntil DateTime @map("covers_until") @db.Timestamptz
  model       String
  generatedAt DateTime @map("generated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  entity KgEntity @relation(fields: [entityId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  owner  User     @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@index([ownerId])
  @@map("kg_entity_digests")
}
// (user_id, entity_id, last_viewed_at) — §9.2's "recently viewed" list. The
// ONE table in this section keyed on `user_id`, not `owner_id` — see
// convention 1 above: which entities a viewer has looked at is a per-viewer
// fact, not an ownership fact (a shared/admin view is out of scope today,
// but the column name is deliberately not `owner_id` so it never implies
// one). A plain `@@unique`, exactly as issue #351's canonical column list
// specifies — NOT the partial unique §10's prose mentions elsewhere;
// nothing about this row is conditional.
model KgEntityView {
  id           String   @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  userId       String   @map("user_id") @db.Uuid
  entityId     String   @map("entity_id") @db.Uuid
  lastViewedAt DateTime @map("last_viewed_at") @db.Timestamptz

  user   User     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  entity KgEntity @relation(fields: [entityId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([userId, entityId])
  @@map("kg_entity_views")
}
// =============================================================================
// `kg_exports` — RDF exports of one owner's graph (issue #386, epic #349;
// docs/specs/ontology.md §18.2)
// =============================================================================
//
// Mirrors `note_exports`: one row per requested render, produced by the
// `kg.export` job into a `managed_by: 'graph'` storage object, expiring 7 days
// after it was requested (the daily sweep deletes the row and the file).
//
// CONTENT-ADDRESSED on `graph_fingerprint` — a SHA-256 over the ontology
// version, the format, and the owner's graph "shape" (max updated_at plus row
// counts of the kg_* tables). `POST /api/graph/exports` reuses an unexpired,
// non-failed row with the same `(owner, format, fingerprint)`, so asking twice
// for an unchanged graph renders once. A plain, NON-unique index — an expired
// or failed row may briefly coexist with a fresh one sharing the key, exactly
// as `note_exports_lookup_idx` allows.
//
// `owner_id` Cascade; `object_id` Restrict (the file goes only after the row
// lets go of it); `job_id` `@unique`/SetNull (the `NoteExport.jobId` shape).
// No hand-written SQL: every constraint here is Prisma-expressible.
// =============================================================================

enum KgExportFormat {
  jsonld
  turtle
  nquads

  @@map("kg_export_format")
}

enum KgExportStatus {
  pending
  running
  ready
  failed

  @@map("kg_export_status")
}

model KgExport {
  id               String         @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  ownerId          String         @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid
  format           KgExportFormat
  status           KgExportStatus @default(pending)
  graphFingerprint String         @map("graph_fingerprint")
  ontologyVersion  String         @map("ontology_version")
  objectId         String?        @map("object_id") @db.Uuid
  // `{ entities, relations, items, evidence, excludedSensitive, bytes }` — counts only.
  stats            Json           @default("{}") @db.JsonB
  // Short and generic; never row data.
  errorMessage     String?        @map("error_message")
  jobId            String?        @unique @map("job_id") @db.Uuid
  expiresAt        DateTime       @map("expires_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  createdAt        DateTime       @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  owner  User           @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  object StorageObject? @relation(fields: [objectId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  job    Job?           @relation(fields: [jobId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  @@index([ownerId, format, graphFingerprint])
  @@index([expiresAt])
  @@map("kg_exports")
}
/// Latest whole-graph layout per owner (spec §22, issue #371). A cache of derived data — never
/// a source of truth; labels are NOT stored (read live, so a renamed or forgotten entity never
/// shows a stale label). `clusters`/`positions` are versioned JSON blobs (`{ version: 1, ... }`)
/// rather than typed columns, produced by the pure `computeLayout()` function and consumed only
/// by `GraphOverviewService`, which joins live entity rows back in at read time.
model KgGraphLayout {
  id      String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  ownerId String @map("owner_id") @db.Uuid

  computedAt DateTime @map("computed_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  nodeCount Int @map("node_count")
  edgeCount Int @map("edge_count")

  clusters  Json @db.JsonB
  positions Json @db.JsonB

  ontologyVersion String @map("ontology_version")

  // max(updated_at) over the owner's kg_entities/kg_relations read by this run — the staleness
  // check `GET /api/graph/overview` compares against a fresh count, never recomputing itself.
  sourceUpdatedAt DateTime? @map("source_updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  owner User @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@index([ownerId, computedAt(sort: Desc)])
  @@map("kg_graph_layouts")
}

// Back-relations onto the platform's User (an `extend model` block holds back-relation fields only).
extend model User {
  // Connected Knowledge (epic #344, issue #351). Every `kg_*` table carries
  // its own `owner_id` and CASCADES — an entity, relation, item, piece of
  // evidence, mention, proposal, merge, distinct-pair or attribute
  // definition has no meaning and no permission path to read it once its
  // owner is gone, the identical reasoning `notes.owner_id`/
  // `transcripts.owner_id` already establish (§10, and there is no
  // `graph:read_any` for the same reason there is no `notes:read_any`).
  // `kgEntityViews` is the one exception: it is keyed on `user_id`, not
  // `owner_id`, because "which entities has THIS user looked at" is a
  // per-viewer fact, not an ownership fact — see the block comment above
  // `KgEntityView`.
  kgEntities      KgEntity[]
  kgEntityAliases KgEntityAlias[]
  kgRelations     KgRelation[]
  kgItems         KgItem[]
  kgEvidence      KgEvidence[]
  kgMentions      KgMention[]
  kgProposals     KgProposal[]
  kgMerges        KgMerge[]
  kgDistinctPairs KgDistinctPair[]
  kgAttributeDefs KgAttributeDef[]
  kgEntityDigests KgEntityDigest[]
  kgEntityViews   KgEntityView[]
  kgGraphLayouts  KgGraphLayout[]

  // #386 — RDF exports of the owner's graph. CASCADES: a disposable,
  // reproducible artefact with its own 7-day expiry, like `note_exports`.
  kgExports       KgExport[]
}

// Back-relations onto the platform's StorageObject (an `extend model` block holds back-relation fields only).
extend model StorageObject {
  // Connected Knowledge (epic #344, issue #351). SetNull — an import's
  // evidence row is a citation into the imported file, and survives losing
  // the object the same way a transcript/note evidence pointer does. See
  // the block comment above `KgEvidence`.
  kgEvidenceImports KgEvidence[]

  // #386 — a rendered graph export (`managed_by: 'graph'`). Restrict, the
  // `noteExportFiles` reasoning: only the export sweep / `kg.purge`, clearing
  // the `kg_exports` reference first, may remove the file.
  kgExportFiles     KgExport[]
}

// Back-relations onto the platform's Job (an `extend model` block holds back-relation fields only).
extend model Job {
  // The `kg_proposals` row this job is extracting (or extracted) into, when
  // `type` is `'kg.extract'` (epic #344, issue #351). Same one-to-one shape
  // as `transcriptNameCheck`/`noteGeneration`/`noteExport`/`transcriptExport`/
  // `backupRun` above, for the same reason: `KgProposal.jobId` is
  // `@unique`/nullable/`SetNull` because a proposal's own review lifetime is
  // independent of `job.history.purge`'s schedule for this row.
  kgProposal KgProposal?

  // The `kg_exports` row this job rendered, when `type` is `'kg.export'`
  // (issue #386). The `noteExport` shape exactly: `KgExport.jobId` is
  // `@unique`/nullable/`SetNull` because an export's own 7-day retention is
  // independent of `job.history.purge`'s schedule for this row.
  kgExport   KgExport?
}
